<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Joint Scaling Law</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For model growth, we also fit the joint scaling law in Equation <a href="#S5.E7" title="In 5.2 Joint Scaling Incorporating Model Size ‣ 5 A Closer Look At The Scaling Behavior ‣ From Acceleration to Saturation: Scaling Behavior of Bootstrapped Language Model Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
Note that there is ambiguity in defining the model size <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> in the context of model growth.
We consider <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> to be the size of the model before growth, and as we keep the growth factor fixed to be 2 in our experiments, the new model size after growth is <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\prime}=2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\prime}=2N</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> differ by a constant factor of 2, which can be absorbed into the coefficient <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#S5.E7" title="In 5.2 Joint Scaling Incorporating Model Size ‣ 5 A Closer Look At The Scaling Behavior ‣ From Acceleration to Saturation: Scaling Behavior of Bootstrapped Language Model Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
Unless stated otherwise, we use <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> in the fitting of the joint scaling law for model growth.
In Figure <a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ D.2 Joint Scaling Law ‣ Appendix D Scaling Laws Details ‣ From Acceleration to Saturation: Scaling Behavior of Bootstrapped Language Model Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, we show the fit of the joint scaling law for stacking.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06548v1/stack_joint.png" id="A4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/182;" width="238" height="182" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A4.F9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Fits of the joint upcycling scaling law of stacking.<span id="A4.F9.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
10% of the collected data points with lowest losses are used for validation (orange
points).</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We provide the fitted coefficients of the joint scaling law in Table <a href="#A4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ D.2 Joint Scaling Law ‣ Appendix D Scaling Laws Details ‣ From Acceleration to Saturation: Scaling Behavior of Bootstrapped Language Model Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
In addition to bootstrapped pretraining, we also show the fitted coefficients for base models trained from scratch on the same dataset as the second stage (code data for CPT, and the same data as the base model for model growth).
For model growth, we consider a growth factor of 2, and fit the parameters with model size <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> before stacking for comparison conveniences.
We further note that the fitted coefficients are produced by fitting the joint scaling law to <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> data points collected, including those used for validation in Figure <a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ D.2 Joint Scaling Law ‣ Appendix D Scaling Laws Details ‣ From Acceleration to Saturation: Scaling Behavior of Bootstrapped Language Model Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A4.T8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 8</span>: </span><span id="A4.T8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Fitted coefficients for joint scaling laws.<span id="A4.T8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Note that for stacked models, we fit the coefficients with model size <math id="A4.T8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> before stacking for comparison conveniences.</span></span></figcaption>
<table id="A4.T8.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A4.T8.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T8.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="A4.T8.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="A4.T8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha/\alpha_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha/\alpha_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A4.T8.6.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="A4.T8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A4.T8.6.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="A4.T8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{3}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A4.T8.6.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="A4.T8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A4.T8.6.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="A4.T8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math></th>
